# Golden set for the oodarag repository corpus.
# expect_sources are substrings matched against a retrieved document's URI or title,
# so they survive chunker changes. Negative cases assert abstention.
{"question": "Why does the robots parser use longest match instead of first match?", "expect_sources": ["robots.py", "LEARNINGS"], "expect_answer_contains": ["9309"], "tags": ["scraper", "spec"]}
{"question": "How does the crawler avoid returning the same page twice?", "expect_sources": ["crawler.py"], "tags": ["scraper", "dedupe"]}
{"question": "What stops a crawl from running forever?", "expect_sources": ["crawler.py"], "tags": ["scraper", "bounds", "known-limitation", "semantic-gap"], "notes": "Known limitation of the offline hashing embedder: the query's most informative term ('forever', idf 4.37) appears nowhere in the corpus, which phrases the concept as 'never terminates' and 'unbounded'. No lexical or feature-hashing method bridges that; a neural embedder would. Kept as a failing case on purpose - it is the measurable argument for a pluggable embedder, and tuning thresholds until it passes would be overfitting the eval."}
{"question": "How are GitHub rate limits handled?", "expect_sources": ["http.py", "github.py", "LEARNINGS"], "tags": ["github"]}
{"question": "How does the GitHub connector avoid re-downloading unchanged files?", "expect_sources": ["github.py"], "expect_answer_contains": ["commit sha"], "tags": ["github", "incremental"], "notes": "Tightened from 'sha', which appeared in 38% of the corpus and was satisfied as readily by 'shared' or 'shape' as by a commit sha. 'commit sha' appears in 6%, and is the mechanism the connector documents: an unchanged head_sha skips the file walk entirely."}
{"question": "Why is reciprocal rank fusion used instead of a weighted score sum?", "expect_sources": ["fusion.py"], "tags": ["retrieval"]}
{"question": "What does maximal marginal relevance do for retrieval?", "expect_sources": ["mmr.py"], "tags": ["retrieval"]}
{"question": "How are secrets kept out of the index?", "expect_sources": ["text.py", "pipeline.py", "base.py"], "tags": ["security"]}
{"question": "Why does every chunk carry a context header?", "expect_sources": ["chunking.py"], "tags": ["chunking"]}
{"question": "How does the embedder work without any network access?", "expect_sources": ["hashing.py"], "tags": ["embedding"]}
{"question": "Why is SQLite used instead of a vector database?", "expect_sources": ["sqlite_store.py"], "tags": ["storage"]}
{"question": "What is the citation contract and how is it enforced?", "expect_sources": ["contract.py"], "tags": ["generation"]}
{"question": "How does the system decide to abstain from answering?", "expect_sources": ["answer.py"], "tags": ["generation"]}
{"question": "What is the capability protocol for probing access?", "expect_sources": ["CAPABILITY-PROTOCOL", "probe.py"], "tags": ["access"]}
{"question": "How does the pipeline detect that vectors are from a stale embedding space?", "expect_sources": ["pipeline.py", "sqlite_store.py"], "tags": ["indexing"], "notes": "Answer expectation 'fingerprint' removed rather than tightened. It is the word the mechanism is named after and the codebase has adopted it: 17 of 83 documents, past the 20% discrimination threshold. Tightening the way 'commit sha' was tightened does not work here - 'embedder_fingerprint', 'index_fingerprint' and \"embedder's fingerprint\" each appear in under 5% of documents and in none of the extractive answers, so each trades a case that passes without discriminating for one that cannot pass. The counterfactual settles it: with pipeline.py and sqlite_store.py excluded the answer still contains the word. Graded on retrieval alone from here. An answer expectation needs a rare literal - an RFC number, 'commit sha' - not a word the code is written in (L63)."}
{"question": "What retrieval metrics does the eval harness report?", "expect_sources": ["metrics.py", "harness.py"], "tags": ["eval"]}
{"question": "What is the capital of France?", "expect_abstain": true, "tags": ["negative"]}
{"question": "Who won the 1998 FIFA World Cup final?", "expect_abstain": true, "tags": ["negative"]}
{"question": "What is the recommended dosage of ibuprofen for adults?", "expect_abstain": true, "tags": ["negative"]}
{"question": "What is the boiling point of mercury?", "expect_abstain": true, "notes": "Regression case. The relevance gate measured its phrase component over every token including stopwords, so the run 'what is the' scored 0.43 and carried this question past the abstention floor on its own. Phrase is now measured over content tokens with a two-token minimum.", "tags": ["negative", "regression", "stopword-phrase"]}
